# Secrets. The first block is the important one.
#
# Nothing in this repository needs a long-lived credential: CI authenticates with
# OIDC, workloads authenticate with IRSA, and the database password is generated by
# RDS and read from Secrets Manager at pod start. If you find yourself wanting to
# commit a key, something in the design has gone wrong.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
credentials
kubeconfig
*-kubeconfig.yaml

# Terraform
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl.bak
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
tfplan
crash.log
crash.*.log
# tfvars can contain account ids and ARNs. The example file is committed; the real
# one is not.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# The provider lock file IS committed. It pins provider versions and checksums for
# every platform, which is what makes an apply reproducible.
!.terraform.lock.hcl

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# Model artifacts and data. These belong in S3, versioned and lifecycle-managed,
# not in git. A repository with a 400MB checkpoint in its history is a repository
# every clone pays for forever.
#
# Anchored with a leading slash, which matters more than it looks. Written as
# "artifacts/", the pattern matches a directory of that name at any depth, and this
# repository has one: terraform/modules/artifacts, which declares the S3 bucket, the
# KMS key and every ECR repository. An unanchored rule silently excludes it, and the
# symptom arrives later as "Module not found" on someone else's first tofu init.
/artifacts/
/data/
*.onnx
*.pt
*.pth
*.pkl
*.joblib
mlruns/
mlartifacts/

# Editors and operating systems
.idea/
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
